! Tetragonal nickel with a fully-relativistic PAW dataset: the anisotropy the
! force theorem cannot compute at all.
!
! **This cell is the point of the relaxed route, not a second example of it.**
! run_force_theorem refuses PAW by name, and correctly: its handoff from the
! collinear leg is a density and nothing else, while a PAW Hamiltonian needs
! ddd_paw, which is built from becsum -- a property of the WAVEFUNCTIONS of a
! run that used a different pseudopotential file with a different number of
! projectors. QE refuses it in the same place (potinit.f90:98). There is no
! handoff in a relaxed run: each direction converges its own becsum from its
! own states, so the refusal does not apply and the quantity exists.
!
! **Two numbers here were wrong on the first attempt and both are worth keeping
! in view, because they are the same trap twice.**
!
!   * starting_magnetization is a FRACTION OF THE VALENCE CHARGE below 1, and
!     this dataset is `spn` -- semicore 3s and 3p included -- so z_valence = 18.
!     The first version wrote 0.4, which seeds 0.4 x 18 = 7.2 mu_B on an atom
!     whose moment is about 0.6. The SCF diverged to an accuracy of 550 and 1700
!     Ry in the two directions and landed 60 Ry apart. 0.05 seeds 0.9 mu_B.
!     This is PLAN.md P84's lesson arriving from the other side: there, a seed at
!     one valence electron's worth started the run on the clamp; here, an
!     innocuous-looking fraction on a high-valence dataset asks for an absurd
!     moment. Neither has an input-time warning that states the seed in mu_B.
!   * the cutoffs are the DATASET'S OWN: its header carries
!     wfc_cutoff = 74.6 Ry and rho_cutoff = 478.2 Ry, and the first version used
!     40/320, a little over half. A semicore PAW dataset at half its suggested
!     cutoff is not a converged calculation of anything.
!
! **This cell has not been run to convergence yet.** It is committed because it
! is the right cell -- a fully-relativistic PAW dataset for a magnetic element,
! which is the regime the force theorem refuses outright -- and because getting
! it wrong twice is worth writing down. At 75 Ry on 18 k-points with a PAW
! spinor it is an hours-long run and wants an idle machine.
!
! fcc nickel drawn on its tetragonal axes (a_fcc = 6.65 bohr gives
! a = 6.65/sqrt(2) = 4.70) and then stretched to c/a = 1.3, which is what makes
! the anisotropy uniaxial and large enough to measure against the noise of two
! total energies. At c/a = 1 the cell is cubic and the uniaxial term vanishes
! by symmetry, which is a control rather than a measurement.
&control
   calculation = 'scf'
/
&system
   ibrav = 6, celldm(1) = 4.70, celldm(3) = 1.30, nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
   noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true., nosym = .true.,
   ecutwfc = 75.0, ecutrho = 480.0,
   occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'mv', degauss = 0.02,
   starting_magnetization(1) = 0.05,
   angle1(1) = 0.0, angle2(1) = 0.0,
/
&electrons
   conv_thr = 1.0e-10
   mixing_beta = 0.3
/
ATOMIC_SPECIES
Ni 58.6934 Ni.rel-pbe-spn-kjpaw_psl.1.0.0.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
Ni 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS automatic
3 3 2 0 0 0
